## Migration Analysis

Your code has **3 patterns** that break on .NET 8:

| API | Problem on .NET 8 | Pattern |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` | Cancellable work loop |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable | Blocking call interruption |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Works, but can't be interrupted via token | Blocking call interruption |

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            // GetConsumingEnumerable blocks until an item is available
            // and throws OperationCanceledException when the token is cancelled,
            // replacing both ManualResetEvent.WaitOne and Thread.Interrupt.
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — nothing to do.
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _queue.Add(item);
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();               // signals the loop to exit
        _thread?.Join(5000);         // wait for graceful shutdown
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _queue.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

---

## What Changed and Why

1. **`BlockingCollection<string>` replaces `Queue` + `ManualResetEvent` + `lock`** — it's thread-safe and blocks on dequeue natively, eliminating the manual signaling/locking.

2. **`CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` replaces `Thread.Interrupt()` + `Thread.Abort()`** — calling `Cancel()` causes `GetConsumingEnumerable` to throw `OperationCanceledException`, cleanly unblocking the thread.

3. **`IDisposable` added** — `CancellationTokenSource` and `BlockingCollection` must be disposed to avoid timer/handle leaks.

---

## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Details |
|---|---|
| **Race in original code** | Your original `WaitOne`/`Reset`/`Dequeue` sequence could lose events — if two `Enqueue` calls happen before `Reset`, only one item is dequeued. `BlockingCollection` fixes this. |
| **`Stop()` doesn't guarantee exit** | Cooperative cancellation only takes effect between items. If `Handle()` is long-running, the thread won't stop until it finishes. Pass the token into `Handle` too if it does blocking work. |
| **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException` mid-chain** | Only catch it at the top-level loop. If `Handle` calls async/cancellable APIs, let the exception propagate up. |
| **Enqueueing after Stop** | `_queue.Add()` will throw `InvalidOperationException` if you call `_queue.CompleteAdding()`. The version above avoids this, but if you add `CompleteAdding()` for a cleaner shutdown, guard `Enqueue` accordingly. |

If `Handle` does long-running work and you need it cancellable too:

```csharp
private void Handle(string item, CancellationToken token)
{
    // Check between expensive steps
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    // ... work ...
}
```
